# The mod loader — design.md §9's load step, and §D8's M7.
#
# **One list, and this is it.** Everything that needs the game — the server, both
# clients, and every probe that generates terrain — calls `load_mods` and reads
# the registries off the `Game` it gets back. Before M7 each of those seven call
# sites imported `core/content.gene` and called four `setup_*` functions in the
# right order; the order is now the mod's own business and the list of mods is
# one line here.
#
# ## The mods are compiled in, and that is M7's honest half
#
# §D5 says "a mod is code loaded at runtime", and this loads no code at runtime:
# `mods/default` is imported like any other module and its `register_all` is
# called. So what M7 has is §9's *API* — a mod is a package, it registers
# through a surface rather than reaching into the engine, its definitions are
# data, and adding a node is a mod change — and not yet §9's *loading*.
#
# That is the right half to do first, and the split is not arbitrary. Runtime
# module loading exists inside the VM (`loadFileModule`) and is not reachable
# from Gene, so exposing it is engine work with a capability model attached
# (§D5: a mod is handed exactly the authorities its manifest declares). Doing it
# before the API existed would have been a loader with nothing worth loading.
#
# **What the split costs today is nothing a user can see, and one thing a
# reviewer should:** the capability model is not enforced, because a compiled-in
# module is not sandboxed by anything. `mods/default` is trusted code in this
# repository. §D5's promise — "a mod that never receives `$fs/WriteDir` cannot
# write a file" — is a claim about the loader that does not exist yet, and this
# file is where it will be true or not.
#
# ## Portable, though the runtime loader will not be
#
# This is `core/` rather than `server/` (where Appendix B put it) because the
# in-tab client generates its own world and therefore needs the same game the
# server has. When runtime loading lands it is a VM capability the web profile
# does not have, and this file splits: a portable list of compiled-in mods, and
# a server-side loader that reads `mods/` off disk.

(mod mods ^profile web)

(import [Game new_game] from "./api.gene")
(import [register_all] from "../mods/default/src/default.gene")

# The game, registered. Called once per process, before anything reads a
# content id — which is every caller's first line, because §2 assigns ids at
# load and nothing means anything before that.
#
# Mod order is the list's order, and with one mod there is nothing to say about
# it. §9's `depends`/`optional_depends` ordering arrives with the second mod and
# with the manifest that declares the dependency; a topological sort over one
# node would be a sort nobody could get wrong and nobody could check.
(fn load_mods [] : Game
  (var game (new_game))
  (register_all game)
  game)
